      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <section class="pt-8 border-t border-slate-800">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>What is the minimum monthly income for the Costa Rica Digital Nomad Visa?</span>
              <span class="text-amber-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              The statutory minimum is $3,000 USD per month for solo applicants and $4,000 USD per month for applicants including a spouse or dependent children. The income must be demonstrated over the preceding 12 consecutive months or validated via a lump-sum bank deposit of $36,000 USD (individual) or $48,000 USD (family).
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>What are the exact bank statement requirements under DGME guidelines?</span>
              <span class="text-amber-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Applicants must submit 12 consecutive months of official bank account statements explicitly showing the applicant's name and consistent offshore deposits. Statements must be accompanied by an official bank certification letter (Carta de Certificación Bancaria) signed by a bank officer, and translated into Spanish by an official Costa Rican certified translator.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>Are digital nomads exempt from local taxes in Costa Rica under Ley 10008?</span>
              <span class="text-amber-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Yes. Under Article 15 of Ley 10008, approved digital nomads receive 100% exemption from Costa Rican Income Tax (Impuesto sobre la Renta) on all foreign-sourced earnings. Holders are also exempt from import duties on computing gear and exempt from compulsory contributions to the CCSS social security system.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>Can you renew the Costa Rica Digital Nomad Visa after the first year?</span>
              <span class="text-amber-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Yes. The initial visa (Estancia) is granted for 12 months and can be renewed for one additional year (24 months total), provided the holder proves they stayed physically inside Costa Rica for at least 180 days during the initial 12-month period.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer font-semibold text-white text-sm">
              <span>Do bank statements need an Apostille seal?</span>
              <span class="text-amber-400 text-lg transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">↓</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Bank statements themselves do not strictly require an Apostille if accompanied by an authorized bank verification letter; however, all official government-issued civil registry documents (such as marriage certificates for spouses and birth certificates for minor dependents) must possess an official Apostille stamp under the Hague Convention.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

